            <div xml:id="d1185">

                <pb facs="0843" n="727" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg727"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.63/33.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1602.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-11-18">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 18, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your No. 65, of October 28, in which you quote the reply of the
                    Foreign Office to your note of October 17 communicating to it the full text of
                    the Department’s telegram No. 297, of October 16, in regard to the decree of
                    August 29 purporting to annul mining titles acquired during the Huerta
                    regime.</p>
                <p>In reply I beg to refer to the Department’s telegram No. 313, of November 4, 4
                    p.m., requesting you to inform the appropriate authorities that the United
                    States Government can not submit to such an application of the decree mentioned
                    as will result in the loss to American citizens of rights or interests acquired
                    legally and in good faith.</p>
                <p>With high appreciation of the able and vigorous attention you have given this
                    matter, I avail [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
